        "text": "NSAIDs-associated ulcers\nWhen an ulcer develops, NSAID should be withdrawn. If continued use of NSAID\nis necessary, refer to specialist. Replace NSAID with paracetamol and/ or with\nnon-drug treatment.\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nomeprazole po\nB\nE\n20mg\ntwice daily\n4 weeks\nNon-Ulcer (functional) dyspepsia\nSymptoms suggestive of ulceration but in absence of organic, metabolic or\nsystemic disease and with negative findings on endoscopy. Symptoms typically\npresent for 6 months presenting either as post-prandial distress syndrome, irritable\nbowel syndrome or epigastric pain syndrome. If symptoms persist despite\ntreatment as per algorithm, refer for specialist evaluation. H.pylori eradication and\nacid suppression are the treatment options. Test for H.pylori and treat if positive\n(see table above) for eradication of H.pylori. For suppression of acid treat as\nfollows:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nomeprazole po\nB\nE\n20mg\nonce daily\n4-8 weeks\nor\nhyoscine po*\nB\nV\n10mg\n3 times a day\n4 weeks\nand\nmetoclopramide po\nC\nV\n10mg\n3 times a day\n4 weeks\nor\nmagnesium\ntriscilicate po\nC\nN\n2 tablets\n3 times a day\nwhen\nnecessary\n*Contraindicated in angle closure glaucoma, prostatic hypertrophy, paralytic ileus\nACUTE DIARRHOEA & ASSOCIATED\nCONDITIONS IN ADULTS\nSee also the sections on HIV-related diarrhoea and diarrhoea in children.\nStools should be examined microscopically and cultured. Pus cells suggest an\ninfective cause.\nAcute Gastro-Enteritis (Food Poisoning)\nAcute diarrhoea +/- vomiting: rehydration and correction of electrolyte disturbance\nis primary intervention, especially in infants, elderly and the very sick. Oral fluids"
