        "shortTitle": "Section 18",
        "longTitle": "PERNICIOUS ANAEMIA",
        "text": "Low dose amitriptyline may be used for the pain. Codeine and related centrally\nacting opioids risk dependency and should be avoided.\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nloperamide\nC\nN\n2-8mg\n2 times a day\nReview\nRefractory cases should be referred.\nCONSTIPATION\nEncourage lifestyle and dietary measures (high fluid intake, fibre intake and\nexercise)\nBulking agent, osmotic laxative, stool softener, stimulant.\nGive laxatives as required but avoid chronic use.\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nglycerine\nsuppository\nrectal\nC\nN\none\nsuppository\nas required\n-\nor\nliquid paraffin po [faecal\nsoftener]\nC\nN\n10-30ml\nas needed\n-\nor\nbisacodyl po [only if no\nabdominal tenderness]\nC\nN\n5 – 10mg\nat night\n-\nChildren: (Liquid paraffin) + stimulant (bisacodyl)\nPregnancy: Use docusate, lactulose, sorbitol, bulking agent. Avoid\nstimulant laxatives and polyethylene glycol\nOpioid-induced: Use an osmotic laxative (sorbitol or lactulose); do not use\nbulking-agent; avoid high fibre diet\nTerminal care: Use Coloxyl with Senna\nPERNICIOUS ANAEMIA\nSuspect diagnosis in macrocytic anaemia. Need to confirm the deficiency. Folic\nacid supplementation is not required.\nGive:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nVitamin B12 inj\nB\nV\nevery 3 months\nlife\n▪See section in chapter on blood."
