      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 1",
        "longTitle": "DIABETES MELLITUS",
        "text": "DIABETES MELLITUS\nClinically, there are four main classifications of diabetes mellitus:\n1.\nType 1 diabetes (T1DM)\nUsually under 30 years but can present at any age, presents acutely, with weight\nloss and ketonuria: Require insulin. Provide dietary advice. Diabetic ketoacidosis\n(DKA) is a medical emergency of type 1 diabetes mellitus and should be managed\nin hospital.\n2.\nType 2 diabetes (T2DM).\nUsually diagnosed in people with overweight, obesity, positive family history of\ndiabetes and occurs more frequently in much older individuals but can occur in\nyounger people.\n3.\nGestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM).\nThis is diabetes that is first diagnosed in the second trimester or third trimester of\npregnancy.\n4.\nOther types of diabetes\nMany other diabetes or hyperglycaemic clinical entities that require specialized\ndiagnoses.\nHence\nearly\nreferral\nto\nDiabetologists/Endocrinologists\nis\nrecommended.\nDietary control and weight loss plays an important part in the management of\ndiabetes mellitus. Many type 2 diabetics are overweight. Reducing body weight\nthrough careful control of energy intake and physical activity like walking helps to\ncontrol the symptoms of diabetes.\nCriteria to diagnose diabetes mellitus\nGenerally, there are four recommended criteria to diagnose diabetes:\n1)\nRandom Blood Glucose (RBG) levels\n2)\nFasting Blood Glucose (FBG) levels\n3)\nOral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT)\n4)\nThe glycated haemoglobin test, known as HbA1c\nGeneral notes:\n•If RBG is greater than 11.1 mmol/l, refer to at least District\nlevel.\n•T1D is usually diagnosed from plasma blood glucose levels in\nage-\nappropriate individuals\n•For T1D patients, use the HbA1c to monitor treatment, rather than for\ndiagnostic purposes.\n\nT2D diagnostic pathway\nKey: 2h = 2 hours. To convert mg/dL to mmol/L of blood glucose divide by 18"
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 2",
        "longTitle": "TYPES OF INSULIN",
        "text": "TYPES OF INSULIN\nInsulin\ntype\nOnset\nPeak\nactivity\n[hrs]\nDuration\n[hrs]\nType of insulin\ne.g.\nBolus\nor\nmeal-time\nInsulins\nRapid\nActing\n(analogue)\n5-15\nmins\n1-1.5\n2-4\nAspart(NoVo\nrapid\n™),\nLispro\n(Humalog\nTM)\nGlulisine(Apidra ™)\nShort acting\n(human\nInsulin)\n30mins\n2 – 4\n5-8\nRegular\nor\nsoluble\n(Actrapid ™)\nBasal\nInsulin\n(Intermediate\nand\nlong-\nacting\ninsulins)\nIntermediate\nacting\n(Human\ninsulin)\n2-4 hrs\n4 – 12\n12- 18\nNeutral\nProtamine\nHagedorn\n(NPH),Isophane\n,\n(Protaphane ™)\nLong-acting\nAnalogue\n2 hrs\n3-9\n6-24\nDetemir\n(Levemir\n™)\nLong Acting\nAnalogue\n2hrs\nNone (flat\nprofile)\n20->24\nGlargine (Lantus ™)\nUltra\nlong\nacting\nanalogue\n30-90\nmins\nnone (flat\nprofile)\nUp to 48\nhours\nInsulin\nDegludec\n(Tresiba ™)\nPre mixed or\nBiphasic\nInsulins\nBiphasic\nHuman\nInsulin\n(BHI)\n30mins\n1st\npeak\nat 2-4hrs ,\n2nd peak\nat\n4-12\nhrs\n12-18 hrs\nRegular 30% + NPH\n70%\nper\nunit\n(Actraphane ™)\nBiphasic\nInsulin\naspart\n30(BIAsp30\n)\n(Analogue)\n15mins\n1st\npeak\nat\n1-\n1.5hrs\n,\n2nd peak\nNone (flat\nprofile)\n20-24hrs\nInsulin Aspart. 30%\n+70% Insulin aspart\nprotamine\n(Novomix30 ™)\nKey: TM denotes trade name or branded product. EDLIZ recommends generic\nprescribing however with insulins, brand names are easier remembered than the\ngeneric names, and indeed, switch between brands may upset on-going\nglycaemic control.\n\nGeneral Insulin dosage guidance and monitoring:\nAt the point of insulin initiation, refer to a specialist Physician/ Endocrinologist\nor consult via Telemedicine.\nIn type 1 diabetes, when initiating insulin treatment, the starting dose is 0.25-\n0.4 units /kg/day. In most T1D patients the basal/bolus regimen is the most\nflexible and physiologic way of delivering insulin. This is also called Multiple\nDaily Injections of insulin (MDII).\nOn calculating the Total Daily Dose (TDDI) of insulin required based on body\nweight, a 50:50 basal/bolus dose split is usually recommended.\nBasal Insulin (50% of Total daily dose of insulin)\nLong acting subcutaneously (SC) once (10pm for analogue insulin) but for the\nhuman insulin NPH (protaphane), doses may need to be split into two equal doses\n12 hours apart\nBasal insulins are normally injected independent of meals, hence the 10pm dosing\nrecommendation.\nAnalogue insulins are associated with easier handling, user-friendly delivery\nsystems, less weight gain, less glycaemic variability and less hypoglycaemia\nrisk.\nBolus Insulin: (50% of the TDDI)\nDedicated to the bolus or meal-time insulin is then divided into three premeal\ndoses to cover the three recommended main meals, breakfast, lunch and supper.\nCan use rapid acting (Actrapid) 30 minutes before meals or analogue insulins eg\nInsulin Aspart (Novorapid) 15 minutes before meals.\nIf however, a meal is to be skipped for whatever reason, the bolus insulin should\nNOT be injected. So, NO MEAL, NO BOLUS!\nA compromise to the MDII regimen or basal/bolus regimen is use of premixed or\nbiphasic insulins. The TDD of insulin should be calculated based on body weight\nas given above.\nPremixed regimens are a less flexible and a less physiologic way of delivering\ninsulin in T1D patients.\n\nMonitoring insulin therapy in diabetes management\nSelf-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) using hand-held blood glucose device\nis mandatory for all T1D patients on insulin therapy\nThe 8-point SMBG daily profile is a useful tool\nThe 3-day profiling of SMBG gives actionable data much as a 7-day profiling\nwould do\nSelf-Monitored Blood Glucose (SMBG) profile testing (the 8-point SMBG\ntemplate).\nKey: BG = blood glucose\nTime\nTargets\nBG test at 0600 hrs OR before breakfast\n4-7 mmol/L\nBG test 2 hrs after breakfast\n4- Less than 9mmol/L\nBG test immediately before lunch\n4-7 mmol/L\nBG test 2 hrs after lunch\n4- Less than 9mmol/L\nBG test immediately before supper (dinner)\n4-7 mmol/L\nBG test 2 hrs after supper (dinner)\nLess than 9 mmol/L\nBG test at bedtime OR at 2200 hrs\n4-9 mmol/L\nBG test at 3AM\n>4mmol/L\nPharmacologic management of T2D patients.\nPatients diagnosed with T2D should be offered dietary counselling and lifestyle\nmodification strategies from the time of diagnosis. However, these non-\npharmacologic measures although good.\nAll T2D first line\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nmetformin po\nB\nV\n500mg\nto\n1000mg\n2 times a day\nindefinitely\n[max 2g/ day]\nContraindications to Metformin use: severe renal failure (eGFR < 30 ml/min),\nsuspected\nor\nconfirmed\nlactic\nacidosis,\nsevere\nliver\ndisease,\nintolerable\ngastrointestinal (GI) upsets even after switch to extended release (ER) Metformin\nand dose reduction.\n\nIf failing to achieve individualized A1c target, add:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\ngliclazide po\nB\nV\n80mg – 160mg\nonce\nOR\ntwice a day\nup to 3 years\nor\nglimepiride po\nA\nE\n1mg – 6mg\nOnce a day\n(with\nbreakfast)\nup to 3 years\nor\n*glibenclamide po\nB\nN\n5mg – 15mg\nOnce\nOR\ntwice a day\nor\nvildagliptin po\nS\nV\n50mg\nonce\nOR\ntwice a day\nindefinitely\nIf still failing to achieve and maintain individualized HbA1c in 3 – 6 months, or\nuse of 3 oral antidiabetic medicines offers no improvement consider basal insulin\ninitiation or refer.\nInsulin therapy in T2D (add to metformin)\nInsulin\nCodes Adult\ndose\nFrequency\nDuration\nNPH\n(Protaphane™)\nB\nV\nunits\nAt bed-time\nIndefinitely\nbut\ntitrate\nto\ntarget\nFBG\nOr\nInsulin detemir\n(Levemir™)\nB\nV\nunits\nAt bed-time\nIndefinitely\nbut\ntitrate\nto\ntarget\nFBG\nOr\nInsulin glargine\n(Lantus™)\nB\nV\nunits\nAt bed-time\nIndefinitely\nbut\ntitrate\nto\ntarget\nFBG\nOr\nInsulin degludec\n(Tresiba™)\nB\nV\nunits\nAt bed-time\nIndefinitely\nbut\ntitrate\nto\ntarget\nFBG\nOr\nActraphane™\n(Premixed)\nB\nV\n6 – 10\nunits\nWith supper\n(dinner)\nOR\ntwice a day\nIndefinitely\nbut\ntitrate\nto\ntarget\nFBG + 2h PPG\nor\nNovoMix30™\n(Premixed)\nB\nV\n6 – 12\nunits\nWith supper\n(dinner)\nOR\ntwice a day\nIndefinitely\nbut\ntitrate\nto\ntarget\nFBG + 2h PPG\nDiscontinue sulphonylureas (glibenclamide and gliclazide) before adding\ninsulin."
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 3",
        "longTitle": "DIABETIC DIET",
        "text": "Monitoring Glycemic control in T2DM.\nThe goals of diabetes care include achieving and maintaining the HbA1c at an\nindividualized target.\nTargets:\nGroup\nTarget\nMost people\nHbA1c < 7%\nYounger\nT2D-\nwith\nno\ncomplications,\nno\nhypoglycaemia risk, good support system and are\nmotivated\nHbA1c < 6.5%\nOlder T2D individuals with no complications\n7-7.5%\nOlder\nindividuals\nwith\nsignificant\ncomplications\n(hypoglycaemia risk, longer duration of diabetes, age-\nrelated incapacities, polypharmacy)\nHbA1c 7.5- 8.5%\nPatients with heart failure, coronary artery disease and chronic kidney disease\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\ndapagliflozin po\nB\nV\n10mg\nonce daily\nLong term\nDIABETIC DIET\nIdeally a dietician should calculate dietary requirements for individual patients.\nAim of diet: to reduce the blood sugar to normal and to maintain a constant blood\nsugar level.\n45-50% of energy intake should be in the form of carbohydrates; the amount of\ncarbohydrates should be consistent from day to day.\nComplex carbohydrates are preferable to simple sugars.\nCarbohydrates and calories should be evenly distributed throughout the day.\nMeals must not be missed. A diabetic on insulin may have snacks between\nmeals.\nAn adequate intake of fibre is important.\nAlcohol is NOT RECOMMENDED in Diabetics.\nSugar and sugar-containing food/drinks should be totally avoided. The only\nexceptions are when a patient feels faint or is ill and cannot eat normally."
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 4",
        "longTitle": "GENERAL ADVICE FOR DIABETICS",
        "text": "Exercise should be encouraged. A snack should be taken before and after\nplaying sport.\nUnrefined carbohydrate, e.g. Roller Meal, wholemeal flour, is preferable to\nrefined starches.\nSpecial preparations for diabetics are safe but not “diet” drinks. 100% fruit\njuices and diet sodas should be totally avoided in Diabetics.\nGENERAL ADVICE FOR DIABETICS\nAll diabetic patients should have a \"medic-alert\" bracelet or necklace\nSyringes / Insulin Storage:\nStore syringes dry.\nDo not sterilize insulin syringes\nuse insulin syringe only once to guarantee sterility\nInsulin should be stored in a cool place.\nInjection technique\nClean and dry skin. Inject subcutaneously at right angles 90 degrees to the skin\ncounting up to 6-10 seconds before removing the needle.\nThe site of injection should be varied (abdomen and thighs, shoulders, gluteal\nregion and back of arm are the most suitable sites).\n.\nFoot Care for Diabetics:\nAdvice about foot care is important: keep feet clean and dry, wear well-fitting\nshoes, and take care to avoid blisters and burns. Healthcare service provider to\nscreen for diabetic foot at each review visit.\nOphthalmological Examinations:\nAt least annually from the time of diagnosis; monitor and record acuities (each\neye separately). If acuity drops, look for cataracts. Refer to an eye\nhospital/specialist.\nPregnant women should be screened for retinopathy in each trimester and\npreconception because retinopathy can get worse in pregnancy.\n\nBlood pressure control:\nGood BP control is essential and is more effective at preventing complications\nthan good glycaemic control. Use combinations of medicines, preferably\nincluding an ACEI, target BP <140/80. If Chronic Kidney Disease (eGFR <\n60ml/hr or proteinuria) target BP < 120/80.\nAspirin and diabetes\nUse for secondary prevention (coronary artery disease, stroke, peripheral\nvascular disease, high cardiovascular risk, chronic kidney disease, peripheral\nneuropathy)\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\naspirin po\nC\nE\n75mg\nonce a day\nindefinitely\nFor those that are allergic to Aspirin or have an intolerance:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nclopidogrel po\nB\nE\n75mg\nonce a day\nindefinitely\nLipid control\nAll type 2 diabetes should be on a statin e.g. Atorvastatin 20mg oral daily for the\nmanagement of hyperlipidaemia.\nType 1 diabetics with complications or above 40 years should be started on a statin.\nSmoking:\nPatients with diabetes should avoid smoking.\nSexual Dysfunction:\nPatients (males and females) MUST always be asked about sexual dysfunction\nand referred accordingly, since it is a marker of vascular disease.\nThey can be offered sildenafil or tadalafil tablets after evaluation for endocrine\ncauses that test for HbA1c, prolactin, TSH and fasting morning total\ntestosterone.\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nsildenafil po\nB\nE\n25mg\nonce a day\nwhen necessary"
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 5",
        "longTitle": "SPECIAL PROBLEMS IN DIABETICS",
        "text": "Oral Care:\nGood oral hygiene should always be encouraged, and patients should have\nannual dental check-ups with a dentist or dental therapists.\nGastrointestinal upset:\ne.g. vomiting, diarrhoea or constipation must be checked often\nas they are an\nindicator of complications. Metformin can cause GIT upset and can be replaced.\nDiabetic Clinics\nAre useful to focus care even at District Hospital level. Six monthly reviews\nshould include eye checks, checking for peripheral neuropathy, checking for\nfoot problems, oral care, sexual dysfunction and BP.\nSPECIAL PROBLEMS IN DIABETICS\nPregnancy\nOral anti-diabetic agents can be used (with Specialist guidance) and very strict\nglycaemic control is necessary. See the chapter on Obstetric and Gynaecological\nconditions.\nInfections and Other Major Illnesses\nBoth types of diabetics (Type 1 and Type 2) may need to be given an increased\ndose of infusion or Basal Bolus Insulin (see insulin therapy in adults). In Type 1\ndiabetes NEVER stop insulin, even if the patient is unable to eat. After a\nhypoglycaemic event correct with 20mls of 50% dextrose and restart the insulin\nat the appropriate dose. Do not stop insulin in a type 1 diabetic.\nInsulin as a BASAL BOLUS REGIMEN is the anti-hyperglycaemic treatment\nof choice in patients admitted to Hospital. There is no role for subcutaneous\nsliding scale in inpatient diabetes management. For critical patients in HDU and\nICU a variable rate intravenous insulin infusion (VRIII) can be used,\nHypoglycaemia and Hypoglycaemic Coma\nAlways educate patients and document in the notes about hypoglycaemic\nsymptoms (hunger, sweating, irritability, etc)..\nClinical presentation:\nAdrenergic features:\nsweating, pallor, tremor, palpitations, tachycardia\nand hunger"
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 6",
        "longTitle": "DEFINITION OF HYPOGLYCAEMIA",
        "text": "Neuroglycopaenic features\nconfusion, seizures & coma\nPatients on oral hypoglycaemic agents and insulin must carry sweets or glucose\ntablets. The patient’s close relatives must also be instructed in the management\nof hypoglycaemic attacks. Metformin cannot cause hypoglycaemia.\nDEFINITION OF HYPOGLYCAEMIA\nCapillary glucose < 3.9 mmol/L\nManagement of hypoglycaemia:\nIn type 1 diabetics, administer glucagon 1mg IM stat.\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nglucagon im\nA\nV\n1mg\nstat\nonce\nIf level of consciousness is depressed, give 50 mL of 50% dextrose by bolus\nintravenous injection. Check blood glucose every 15 minutes. Repeat 25 mL of\n50% dextrose intravenously, every 15 minutes until blood glucose is 4 mmol/L.\n5% dextrose: infused slowly (50-60 mL per hour) after injection of 50% dextrose\nand titrated according to capillary plasma glucose levels. Sulfonylurea-induced\nhypoglycaemia may be prolonged and glucose infusions may be needed for 2-3\ndays. The patient should take oral carbohydrate as soon as possible after the initial\nmanagement with 50% dextrose.\nIn the event of confusion or coma in a diabetic on treatment and in the absence\nof reliable blood sugar readings, there should be no hesitation in administering a\ntrial injection of intravenous dextrose.\nIf the above are unavailable, small quantities of sugar or preferably glucose, may\nbe placed inside the cheeks and will be eventually swallowed or absorbed through\nthe buccal mucosa.\nIf awake and normal conscious level give fast acting sugars eg glucose tablets,\n100% juices, sweets or consider giving IV dextrose as above.\nSURGERY\nDiabetic patients requiring surgery SHOULD ALWAYS BE THE FIRST ONES\nON THE SURGERY LIST and are best cared for by Specialists. Refer wherever\npossible.\nIn the case of diabetic patients on oral agents, stop the oral medicines and\ncommence Basal Bolus Regimen."
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 7",
        "longTitle": "SURGERY",
        "text": "Neuroglycopaenic features\nconfusion, seizures & coma\nPatients on oral hypoglycaemic agents and insulin must carry sweets or glucose\ntablets. The patient’s close relatives must also be instructed in the management\nof hypoglycaemic attacks. Metformin cannot cause hypoglycaemia.\nDEFINITION OF HYPOGLYCAEMIA\nCapillary glucose < 3.9 mmol/L\nManagement of hypoglycaemia:\nIn type 1 diabetics, administer glucagon 1mg IM stat.\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nglucagon im\nA\nV\n1mg\nstat\nonce\nIf level of consciousness is depressed, give 50 mL of 50% dextrose by bolus\nintravenous injection. Check blood glucose every 15 minutes. Repeat 25 mL of\n50% dextrose intravenously, every 15 minutes until blood glucose is 4 mmol/L.\n5% dextrose: infused slowly (50-60 mL per hour) after injection of 50% dextrose\nand titrated according to capillary plasma glucose levels. Sulfonylurea-induced\nhypoglycaemia may be prolonged and glucose infusions may be needed for 2-3\ndays. The patient should take oral carbohydrate as soon as possible after the initial\nmanagement with 50% dextrose.\nIn the event of confusion or coma in a diabetic on treatment and in the absence\nof reliable blood sugar readings, there should be no hesitation in administering a\ntrial injection of intravenous dextrose.\nIf the above are unavailable, small quantities of sugar or preferably glucose, may\nbe placed inside the cheeks and will be eventually swallowed or absorbed through\nthe buccal mucosa.\nIf awake and normal conscious level give fast acting sugars eg glucose tablets,\n100% juices, sweets or consider giving IV dextrose as above.\nSURGERY\nDiabetic patients requiring surgery SHOULD ALWAYS BE THE FIRST ONES\nON THE SURGERY LIST and are best cared for by Specialists. Refer wherever\npossible.\nIn the case of diabetic patients on oral agents, stop the oral medicines and\ncommence Basal Bolus Regimen."
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 8",
        "longTitle": "FLUID REPLACEMENT (ADULTS)",
        "text": "If NIL BY MOUTH\n■\nBasal: 50% of Total daily dose insulin (TDDI)\n\nLong-acting insulin at bedtime or morning\n\nOr Intermediate insulin twice a day (50/50 or 2/3 am and 1/3 pm)\n\nOr Insulin INFUSION (preferred if prolonged NPO, ICU or ketosis prone)\n■\nCorrection\nRapid acting insulin sc every 4 hours\nOr Regular insulin sc every 6 hours\ncheck blood sugar - if less than 5 mmol/L set up 5% dextrose infusion to run\nslowly,\nDo hourly sugar checking intraoperatively\ncheck blood sugar post-operatively and if nil per mouth administer Basal and\nCorrection Insulin only.\nHyperglycaemic Coma & Pre-coma (Adults)\nPass a nasogastric tube and allow free drainage in the unconscious or\nsemiconscious patient. Search for and treat infections promptly.\nFLUID REPLACEMENT (ADULTS)\nDiabetic Ketoacidosis DKA- always give insulin first at 0.1 units per kg\nfollowed by fluids.\nHyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS)- always give fluids first followed by\ninsulin at 0.05 units per kg per hour\nSodium chloride 0.9% is the recommended fluid (Ringers lactate can be used as\nwell); as much as 8 litres may be required in 24 hours: The schedule given is a\nguide. Be flexible\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose– fluid rate\nsodium\nchloride\n0.9%\niv\ninfusion\nC\nV\nfirst litre\nsecond litre\nthird litre\nfourth litre\nfifth litre\nover 1 hour\nover 2 hours\nover 4 hours\nover 6 hours\nover 8 hours\nGive subsequent litres of sodium chloride 0.9% every 8 hours. Monitor closely\nduring the period of infusion and modify accordingly, e.g. take into account skin\nturgor, peripheral perfusion and urine output."
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 9",
        "longTitle": "DKA INSULIN MANAGEMENT (ADULTS)",
        "text": "CAUTION: Fluid overload is dangerous in elderly, heart failure and CKD\npatients.\nThe above regimen may need to be modified depending on the state of hydration\nor the cardiovascular status of the patient.\nBeware of hypernatremia by monitoring electrolytes and be prepared to change\nto a hypotonic solution, e.g. 5% dextrose if appropriate, or half sodium chloride\n0,9% (prepared by diluting normal saline by 50% with water for injection).\nWhen blood sugar falls to 14mmol/L change to dextrose 5% (or 10%) (or if\nurinary ketones can be measured), set up 5% dextrose (or 10%) infusion if\nketones moderate or strong and blood sugars <14mmol/L.\nPotassium Replacement\nIn conditions where blood potassium levels cannot be determined, add to\nintravenous fluid:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nadd\npotassium chloride iv\ninfusion\nB\nV\n20mmol with every litre after the first litre.\nIncrease to 40mmol / litre given over 8hrs.\n\nWhere serum potassium levels are available start replacement:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\npotassium\nchloride\niv\ninfusion\nB\nV\n20mmol / litre as soon as insulin has been\nstarted.\n\nAssess serum potassium regularly and adjust replacement as needed to\nmaintain potassium at 4.0-5.0mmol/per litre.\n\nContinue with oral replacement for one week if not in renal failure:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\npotassium chloride po\nB\nV\n–\n1200mg\ntwice a day\n7 days\nDKA INSULIN MANAGEMENT (ADULTS)\nPatients preferentially to be managed with protocol 1 in a High Care ward, with\nappropriate monitoring.\nPROTOCOL 1: - continuous intravenous infusion (fixed rate intravenous insulin\ninfusion):\nGive a bolus intravenous injection of 0.1 units/kg short acting insulin followed\nby a continuous intravenous infusion.\n\nMix 50 units short acting insulin in 200 mL isotonic saline - thus 4 mL solution\ncontains 1 unit of insulin\nInitial infusion: 0.1 unit/kg/hr (usually 5-7 units per hour: 20-28 mL/hr)\nWhen plasma glucose < 14 mmol/L, change the IV fluid to 5% dextrose (or 10%\nif available). Aim to get rid of ketonuria. Once ketones are no longer there, the\ninsulin doses can be reduced as the blood glucose is also controlled. NOTE:\nKetonemia takes longer to clear than hyperglycaemia and combined insulin and\nglucose (and K+) are needed to ensure clearance of ketonemia. Avoid focusing\non glucose control alone!\nPROTOCOL 2: -If no access to insulin infusion, hourly intramuscular or\nintravenous bolus injections:\nHourly doses: 0.1 unit/kg either by intramuscular or intravenous injection with\nhourly bedside capillary glucose level until ketones negative. Change IV fluids\nto 10% dextrose once sugar less than 14 mmol/L\nPROTOCOL 3:\nInitially given by intramuscular injection (be careful not to inject into\nsubcutaneous fat, use intramuscular needles and in very obese patients use the\ndeltoid region), see below:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nsoluble insulin im\nB\nV\n10units\nimmediately, then\n5units\nhourly\nuntil\nblood\nsugar\ndown\nto\n14mmol/L\nWhen the blood sugar is 14mmol/L or less and the clinical condition shows clear\nimprovement, change to subcutaneous administration but continue to monitor\nblood sugar hourly until the level ceases to fall (the intramuscular injection may\ncontinue to act for some hours through a depot effect). Then give insulin\naccording to Basal Bolus regimen.\nCommon problems with using the Sliding Scale Only (No longer recommended.\nBasal Bolus Regimen Preferred)\n1- Is reactive rather than proactive\n2- Often mismatched with changes in patient’s insulin sensitivity\n3- It does not meet the physiologic needs of the patient\n4- Leads to insulin stacking"
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 10",
        "longTitle": "Metabolic conditions in children",
        "text": "⮩\nBlood Sugar\n[mmol/L]\nSoluble Insulin\n>16\n12 units\n>12-16\n8 units\n>8-12\n4 units\n<8\n0.5 units\nSliding scales using URINE glucose tests are unreliable - avoid.\nMetabolic conditions in children\nHypoglycaemia in Children\nCommon causes of hypoglycaemia include excessive insulin dose, skipping meals,\nand exercise. In children who are awake- give 100% juice, glucose tablet or a\nsweet, to increase the glucose fast. Thereafter maintain glucose level with a\ncarbohydrate e.g. sandwich\nIf unconscious give:\nMedicine\nCodes\nPaed dose\nFreq\nDuration\n10% dextrose iv\nA\nN\n2mg/kg/hr\nor\nglucagon iv\nA\nV\n<25kg - 0.5mg\n>25kg- 1mg\nstat\nonce\nMaintain with a dextrose containing fluid until the child is able to eat or drink.\nCheck glucose level every 20 min until normal.\nChildren with Hyperglycemic States\nHyperglycaemic Coma and Pre-coma (Children)\nNB. Refer to the appropriate local guideline used by paediatricians for Diabetic\nketoacidosis (DKA) and hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS) at the\nquaternary hospitals.\nPriorities:\nFluid replacement\nElectrolyte / acid-base monitoring\nInsulin therapy\nBlood glucose monitoring\nUse\nblood\nsugar\nreagent\nstrips\nor\nglucometer\nreadings. Do not rely totally\non these readings- also use\nclinical judgement.\n\nFluid Replacement\n\nApproximately 200 ml/kg in 24 hours is required for rehydration.\n\nStart with rapid infusion of:\nMedicine\nCodes\nRate\nsodium\nchloride\n0.9% iv infusions\nTotal\nvolume\n=\n200ml/kg in 24 hours\nC\nV\n20ml/kg fast, then\n½ the remaining volume in 8hrs, then\n½ the remaining volume in 16hrs.\nand\npotassium\nchloride\ninfusion\nB\nV\nadd 20mmol/L after the initial 20mg/kg\nfast infusion.\nMonitor glucose levels hourly: when the blood sugar is less than 15mmol/l\nchange to:\nMedicine\nCodes\nRate\nhalf strength Darrows\nwith 5% dextrose iv\ninfusion *\nC\nV\nsee section on iv fluid replacement\nand\npotassium chloride iv\nB\nV\n20mmol per litre of the ½ Darrows/ dextrose\nsolution\n* Made up by adding 50mls of 50% dextrose to 1 litre ½ Darrow’s with 2.5%\ndextrose.\n\nMonitor U/E 2-4 hourly watching the potassium levels.\nInsulin Therapy (Children)\nMedicine\nCodes\nDose\nRate\nsoluble insulin iv\n(initial\n–\ncontinuous\ninfusion)*\nB\nV\n0.1units/\nkg/ hr\nuntil\nblood\nsugar\nfalls\nbelow\n15mmol/L, then\n(*e.g. make up infusion of\ninsulin in normal saline)\n0.05units/\nkg/ hr\nuntil condition stabilises, then\nsoluble insulin sc\n(maintenance)\nB\nV\n0.75 – 1unit/kg/day in 3 divided doses before\nmeals for one day, then\nsoluble + isophane sc\nB\nV\napply the rule of thirds, (2/3 of the total daily\ndose in the morning and 1/3 in the evening).\ne.g. 30kg child\nInitial fluid requirement = (30 x 200mls) = 6 Litres\n\n▪\ninitial bolus of 600mls normal saline fast\n▪\nfollowed by (5.4L / 2) = 2.7L in next 8 hrs and 2.7L in next 16hrs\nInitial insulin = (30 x 0.1) = 3 units iv continuous infusion,\n▪\nslowing to (30 x 0.05) = 1.5units/hr when glucose < 15 mmol/L\n▪\nwhen stable (+ 0.75 – 1 unit x 30kg / day) e.g. 24 units per day (24/3) =\n8 units 3 times a day before meals\n▪\nthen after 24hrs, 24 units isophane and soluble insulin/day: 2/3 isophane:\n10 units am6 units am\n1/3 soluble:\n6 units am; 4 units am\nHoneymoon period\nIn the months after initial diagnosis insulin requirements may decline to less than\n0.5 unit/kg/day as the pancreas continues to produce some endogenous insulin.\nRequirements invariably revert to higher doses as endogenous insulin levels\ndecline. Explain the concept to the patient or relatives.\nNote: Diet is important in children but attempts at too rigid control may prove to\nbe counterproductive. The child living with diabetes should be allowed to indulge\nin normal activities at school. Teachers need to be informed about the condition\n(Loco parentis should apply).\nEndocrinological Conditions.\nNB. In general most endocrinology patients are best referred or discussed on a\ntelemedicine platform with Endocrinology Specialists.\nEndocrine diseases include young hypertension patients below 44 years, pituitary\ndisease, thyroid diseases, parathyroid gland, adrenal diseases, testicular and\novarian failure, calcium disorders, obesity, neuroendocrine tumours, other\nelectrolyte problems such as hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia and puberty and\ngrowth failure.\nThyroid Disease\nAnyone with Goitre needs referral for work up.\nPoints in Management\nA patient with a visible goitre will need a complete history and examination.\nInvestigations include a TSH. Ultrasound of the thyroid by a qualified thyroid\nultrasonographer and/or radiologist will be useful to exclude features suspicious of\ncancer.\n\nThe initial treatment for thyrotoxicosis includes propranolol while awaiting full\nthyroid results (TSH, FT4 and FT3).\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\npropranolol po\nB\nE\n40 –160 mg\n3 times a day\n-\nGraves’ disease\n\nTreat initially with anti-thyroid medicines:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\ncarbimazole po\nB\nE\n20 -60mg\n[0.5 mg/kg]\nafter\ninitiating\ntreatment\nrefer\nto\nspecialist for titration\nCAUTION: Carbimazole induce bone marrow suppression; advise patient to\nreport sore throat or other signs of infection. Stop medicine immediately if\nneutropenic. Minor rashes are not an indication to stop treatment.\nCheck thyroid function at 5-6 weeks and if normalised, gradually reduce the dose\nto the lowest that will maintain euthyroidism. Continue carbimazole for one year\nfrom time of stabilisation. If poor response, relapse or clinically very severe, refer\nfor radioiodine or surgery.\nNB: after radio-iodine therapy for Graves’ disease, long-term follow up is\nessential to detect late hypothyroidism that might otherwise remain neglected and\nuntreated.\nToxic Nodular Goitre [including toxic adenoma]\nWhere available Radioiodine at the appropriate centre is recommended, particularly\nin older patients and those with other medical problems.\nSurgery is particularly suitable for those with a large goitre. As radioiodine may take\nthree months or longer to produce a clinical effect, propranolol may be continued\nuninterrupted and carbimazole may be restarted. Patients must be rendered euthyroid\nprior to thyroidectomy by use of anti-thyroid medicines.\n\nAqueous iodine oral solution may be administered for 10-14 days before\nthyroidectomy:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nAq. iodine solution\n(Lugol’s iodine)\n130mg iodine/ml\nA\nN\n0.1 – 0.3ml\ndiluted\nin\nmilk\ntimes\na\nday\n10-14\ndays\nbefore\nsurgery"
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 11",
        "longTitle": "Hypothyroidism",
        "text": "Hypothyroidism\nExcept in iodine deficient areas, this is treated by thyroid hormone replacement\nwhatever the cause:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nthyroxine po\nB\nV\n50 - 100mcg\ninitially\nonce a day\n4 weeks, then\nincrease by 25 - 50mcg every four weeks as necessary until euthyroid\nStart at 25mcg/day in the elderly or those with heart disease.\nTypical adult replacement dose is 1.6mcg/kg/day [i.e. 100mcg daily].\nLarger doses are needed in infancy [10 - 15mcg/kg/day] and childhood\n[4mcg/kg/day].\nClose monitoring of clinical response including weight monitoring and thyroid\nfunction tests (T4, TSH) is essential.\nHypoadrenalism\nMay be primary (Addison’s disease) or secondary to pituitary failure, e.g. as a\nresult of surgical or irradiation ablation of the pituitary gland.\nRequires specialist physician/endocrinologist investigation and advice on how to\ntreat the patient.\nSurgery or illness necessitates an increase in corticosteroid cover generally in the\nform of hydrocortisone parenterally in the acute phase, followed by oral\nprednisolone in a higher than usual dosage as the condition improves. Patients on\nlong term corticosteroid who develop infection or are subjected to surgery also\nrequire additional steroid cover as above.\nAdrenal Crisis (Systolic BP less than 100mmHg),\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nhydrocortisone inj\nC\nV\n100mg\nstat\n0.9% Normal Saline 1 litre in first hour, check supine and erect BP"
      },
      {
        "shortTitle": "Section 12",
        "longTitle": "Hypoadrenalism",
        "text": "Hypothyroidism\nExcept in iodine deficient areas, this is treated by thyroid hormone replacement\nwhatever the cause:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nthyroxine po\nB\nV\n50 - 100mcg\ninitially\nonce a day\n4 weeks, then\nincrease by 25 - 50mcg every four weeks as necessary until euthyroid\nStart at 25mcg/day in the elderly or those with heart disease.\nTypical adult replacement dose is 1.6mcg/kg/day [i.e. 100mcg daily].\nLarger doses are needed in infancy [10 - 15mcg/kg/day] and childhood\n[4mcg/kg/day].\nClose monitoring of clinical response including weight monitoring and thyroid\nfunction tests (T4, TSH) is essential.\nHypoadrenalism\nMay be primary (Addison’s disease) or secondary to pituitary failure, e.g. as a\nresult of surgical or irradiation ablation of the pituitary gland.\nRequires specialist physician/endocrinologist investigation and advice on how to\ntreat the patient.\nSurgery or illness necessitates an increase in corticosteroid cover generally in the\nform of hydrocortisone parenterally in the acute phase, followed by oral\nprednisolone in a higher than usual dosage as the condition improves. Patients on\nlong term corticosteroid who develop infection or are subjected to surgery also\nrequire additional steroid cover as above.\nAdrenal Crisis (Systolic BP less than 100mmHg),\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nhydrocortisone inj\nC\nV\n100mg\nstat\n0.9% Normal Saline 1 litre in first hour, check supine and erect BP"
      }
