        "shortTitle": "Section 1",
        "longTitle": "PREVENTION OF BLINDNESS",
        "text": "PREVENTION OF BLINDNESS\n80% of blindness can be prevented by:\nproper diet (Vitamin A and proteins)\npersonal and environmental hygiene\nmeasles immunisation\nearly treatment of eye diseases by qualified health personnel including cataract\nsurgery and early diagnosis and treatment of glaucoma\nappropriate management of STIs in pregnant mothers and their sexual partners\nearly referral of serious eye diseases and injuries\ntetracycline eye ointment in the new-born child’s eyes (Crede Prophylaxis)\nearly detection and appropriate management of diabetes – stringent control of\nblood sugar. All diabetic patients should have screening of eye complications by\nan ophthalmologist regularly starting from diagnosis of the disease.\nearly detection and appropriate management of hypertension\ncreation of community awareness on the dangers of using herbal medicines in\nthe eye\npersistent conjunctival growths should be referred to an ophthalmologist early,\nin case it is due to Ocular Surface Squamous Neoplasia\nHIV positive patients must be screened for eye complications by an ophthalmic\nnurse or ophthalmologist if symptomatic\npatients with allergic conjunctivitis must be discouraged from rubbing eyes and\nappropriate treatment should be used e.g. antihistamines. Avoid use of topical\nsteroids since they can cause glaucoma in steroid responders and early cataract\nformation.\n“Healthy bodies, healthy eyes!”\nOrganic headaches such as migraine and cluster headaches do NOT occur\nbecause of eyestrain. See the chapter on Nervous System Conditions for\nmanagement of these conditions.\nExcessive use of eyes does NOT harm them, and “bad eyes” do NOT result from\noveruse.\nMANAGEMENT OF EYE CONDITIONS\nDiagnose and refer urgently most of the following conditions:\nunexplained vision loss; or visual disturbance\nglaucoma: high pressure in the eye"
