        "shortTitle": "Section 5",
        "longTitle": "XEROPHTHALMIA/ VITAMIN A DEFICIENCY",
        "text": "At hospital: Patients suspected of having acute angle closure glaucoma must be\nstarted on pilocarpine 2% or 4% drops in both eyes four times a day.\nOther measures which can be taken include: application of central corneal\npressure with muscle hook or gonioprism. This can abort recent onset of acute\nangle closure glaucoma.\nInstil topical beta blocker e.g. Timolol 0.5%\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nacetazolamide po\nA\nN 250-500mg\nstat, then\n8 hourly\nreview\nRefer to an Eye Specialist within 24 hours.\nXEROPHTHALMIA/ VITAMIN A DEFICIENCY\nPreventive measures include promotion of breast-feeding, measles immunisation,\nVitamin A supplementation, foods rich in Vitamin A [carrot, mango, pumpkin,\nand paw-paw]. To all children with signs and symptoms of xerophthalmia and/or\nmeasles, give and refer:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nvitamin A po\nC\nV\n200,000 iu [<1yr\n= 100, 000 iu]\nsingle dose\nDay 1, Day 2 and\nrepeat\nafter\nweeks\nNutritional rehabilitation is indicated.\n\nTable 21.1 Differential Diagnosis of a Red Eye (Atraumatic)\nCondition\nRedness\nPain\nBlurred vision\nDischarge\nPupil size/ shape/\nreaction to light\nVisual\nacuity\nRefer\nAcute\nclosed angle\nglaucoma\nYes\nMax.\naround\nlimbus\none\nor\nboth eyes\nYes.\nSevere\n+\nheadaches + nausea\n+ vomiting.\nYes. Severe +\nhalos\naround\nlights\nNo\nDilated.\nFixed.\nDecreased\nYes\nConjunctivi\ntis\nYes\nGeneralised\nboth\neyes usually\nYes\nGritty\nPhotophobia\nNo\nYes.\nMaybe\ncopious.\nNormal\nNormal\nOnly\nif\nno\nresponse\nOr\ncopious\ndischarge\nCorneal\nulcer\nYes\nMax. around the\nlimbus more near\nthe\nsite\nof\nthe\nulcer, usually one\neye.\nYes.\nPricking.\nPhotophobia. Stains\nwith\nfluorescein\nstrips.\nYes\nYes,\nin\nbacterial\n/fungal ulcers\nNo, in viral /\ntraumatic\nulcers\nNormal\nDecreased\n. Depends\non the site\n/\nsize\nof\nthe ulcer\nYes\nIritis/uveiti\ns\nYes\nMax.\naround\nlimbus,\none\nor\nboth eyes\nYes.\nDeep\npain\nworsens\non\nthe\nmoving\neye.\nPhotophobia\nYes\nNo\nIrregular,\nsmall,\nsluggish reaction to\nlight\nDecreased\nYes\nOcular\nYes\nYes\nUsually no when Watery\ndue Normal\nNormal\nYes\n\nsurface\nsquamous\nneoplasia\nLocalised\nto\ninvolved\nconjunctiva\nand\nredness does not\nclear\nwith\nshort\ncourse\nof\nmild\ntopical steroid\nOut of proportion to\nsize of growth\nsmall\nforeign\nbody\nsensation\nwhen\nsmall\nPterygium\nYes\nLocalised on the\nnasal\nside\nof\nlimbus\nwith\nconjunctiva\ngrowing\nover\ncornea\ntowards\nthe\ncentre.\nRespond to mild\ntopical\nsteroid\ndrops / ointment\nYes,\nespecially\nwhen inflamed\nUsually no\nNo\nunless\ninflamed\nNormal\nNormal\nYes"
