        "longTitle": "25. EAR NOSE AND THROAT DISORDERS",
        "text": "25. EAR NOSE AND THROAT\nDISORDERS\nACUTE OTITIS MEDIA (AOM)\nRECURRENT ACUTE OTITIS MEDIA\nACUTE MASTOIDITIS\nOTITIS MEDIA WITH EFFUSION\nFOR MANAGEMENT REFER TO SPECIALIST\nCHRONIC SUPPURATIVE OTITIS MEDIA\nOTITIS EXTERNA\nMALIGNANT OTITIS EXTERNA\nKERATOSIS OBLITERANS\nRHINOLOGY\nACUTE RHINOSINUSITIS\nCHRONIC RHINOSINUSITIS\nALLERGIC RHINOSINUSITIS\nFUNGAL RHINOSINUSITIS-INVASIVE\nNON-INVASIVE FUNGAL RHINOSINUSITIS\nOBSTRUCTIVE SLEEP APNOEA FROM ADENOTONSILLAR\nHYPERTROPHY\nTONSILLITIS-ACUTE\nEPISTAXIS\nCSF RHINORRHOEA\nCROUP\nACUTE CERVICAL LYMPHADENITIS\n\nAcute Otitis Media (AOM)\nPatient presents with fever, chills and irritability. Most common in those under 2\nyears of age. Examination shows an irritable child, tympanic membrane inflamed\nand bulging.\nNatural history\n60% resolve in 24hrs\n80% by 48 hrs\n88% 4-7 days\nOME 63% resolve after 2 weeks 40% remaining after one month\n20% remaining by 3 months\nOrganisms that are involved in Acute Otitis Media\nStreptococcus pneumonia (35%), Haemophilus influenzae (23%), Moraxella\ncatarrhalis (14%) form the majority.\nTreatment\nAvoid risk factors-breastfeed for more than 6 months; avoid parental smoking,\nencourage vaccination, provide good nutrition and encourage early attendance to\nday care.\nAnalgesia and supportive care\nIndications for giving antibiotics\nAOM under 6 months\nSevere AOM-temperature (axillary) > 39.5ºC"
