        "shortTitle": "Section 10",
        "longTitle": "CARE OF THE TERMINALLY ILL",
        "text": "Muscle spasm\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nPaed dose\nFrequency\nDuration\ndiazepam po\nB\nV\n2-10mg\n0.04-\n0.8mg/kg\nevery\n6-8\nhrs\nreview\nor\nbaclofen po\nB\nN\n5-10mg\n5mg\ntimes\na\nday\nreview\nNEUROPATHIC PAIN: see under adjuvant analgesics\nRESPIRATORY\nShortness of Breath\nMedicine\nAdult dose\nPaed dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nmorphine po\n2.5-5mg\n0.1-0.2mg/kg\nevery 4 hours\nreview\nor\ndiazepam po*\n5-10mg\n0.04-0.05mg/kg\nonce a day\nreview\nor\ndexamethasone po\n4-8mg\n0.08-0.3mg/kg\nonce a day\nreview\nor\nprednisolone po\n5-20mg\n0.1-2mg/kg\nonce a day\nreview\n*diazepam: frequency can be increased to twice or thrice a day, as per\nassessment\nCARE OF THE TERMINALLY ILL\nThis is a part of palliative care when the illness has reached an irreversible state\nand soon will result in death or a state of permanent unconsciousness from which\nthe patient is unlikely to recover. Care is focussed on keeping the patient as\ncomfortable as possible and curative approaches are discontinued. This usually\nrequires a good multidisciplinary team (MDT) approach. Good palliative care can\ngreatly relieve the mental and physical suffering of terminally ill patients and their\nfamilies.\nPsychological support\nA full explanation of the illness, the treatment and expected physical symptoms\nshould be discussed (often on several occasions). It is important that health\nworkers be available to provide continuing support. Fear and anxiety about dying,\npain and other distressing symptoms are common, and patients may become\ndepressed.\n\nManagement includes:\nhonest answers should be provided to all questions asked about the illness, and\ndelivered with compassion\ntaking time to allow patients and their family to share their problems and\nconcerns\nproper control of pain and other symptoms\nManagement of physical symptoms\nPain control – see text above\nNausea and vomiting – see text above\nLoss of appetite - may be due to many causes including medications. If possible,\nidentify and treat the cause.\nGood oral care and adequate hydration should be\nensured, using simple mouthwashes.\nDifficulty in swallowing – may be due to pharyngeal or oesophageal obstruction,\nor thrush. Identify & treat the cause if possible. A feeding tube might help. Good\noral hygiene and hydration.\nDiarrhoea – may be due to constipation with overflow, or excessive use of\nlaxatives. Use loperamide, codeine or morphine.\nShortness of breath – may be due to infections or pleural effusions. This is one of\nthe most feared symptoms and every effort should be made to alleviate it. The\ncause should be established and treated if possible. A calming presence of a\nrelative/carer, propping the patient up, and the use of low-dose morphine (2.5-5mg\nevery 4 hours – or a 25% increase in dose currently being given for analgesia) and\nmild sedatives may help. Oxygen is only indicated if there is cyanosis.\nTerminal restlessness - can be defined as agitated delirium with cognitive\nimpairment during the dying process.\nDuring the last hours of a person’s life, carers should focus on minimising pain,\nreducing respiratory secretions (e.g. atropine 0.6mg sc 4hrly, hyoscine 5-10mg po\nevery 6-8hrs) and observing for urinary problems (retention or incontinence).\nMedicines should be rationalised, stopping all but those relevant to terminal\nsymptoms/signs. Intravenous fluids should be discouraged (or discontinued) as\nthey aggravate terminal respiratory problems. If symptoms and distress are not\neasily controlled, it may be appropriate to use terminal sedation. Families should\nalways be informed about the dying process and consulted on medicine and fluid\nrationalization.\n\nPalliative care emergencies (address immediately then refer appropriately or\nmanage as per MDT):\nSpinal cord compression: as above and refer appropriately\nMalignant bowel obstruction: refer appropriately\nSevere bleeding\nSuperior vena cava syndrome\nN.B Use of syringe drivers in palliative care can be indicated when the patient\ncannot tolerate oral medications for various reasons e.g. continuous vomiting.\nSyringe drivers should be initiated by trained staff."
