        "shortTitle": "Section 2",
        "longTitle": "Anti-hypertensives",
        "text": "Medicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nmirabegron\n(modified\nrelease)\npo\nS\nE\n50mg\nonce daily\nTrial for 2-3 months.\nStop if no benefit after trial\nperiod.\nConstipation\nConstipation describes harder than normal stools, infrequent or increased time\nbetween bowel movements, and/or difficulty/pain on defecation.\nConstipation risk increases with age, as bowel motility slows. Immobility is an\nimportant risk factor for constipation, as are other chronic conditions (e.g.\nhypothyroidism, parkinsonism). Many commonly prescribed medications can\ncontribute to constipation in older people (e.g. opiate analgesia, iron supplements,\ncalcium-channel blockers). Paradoxically, constipation can become so severe that\nit causes faecal impaction with overflow diarrhoea, which is the most common\ncause of faecal incontinence in older people – hence, anti-diarrhoeal medications\nshould be avoided in older people.\nDue to the multi-factorial nature of constipation, management must be equally\nmulti-faceted, including hydration, mobilisation, high-fibre diet, and medication\nreview. Where pharmacological intervention is required, this can be achieved with\noral stimulant laxatives (e.g. senna, bisacodyl), or treatment per rectum (e.g.\nglycerin suppositories, enemas).\nAnti-hypertensives\nPrescribe with caution due to increased risk of postural hypotension, side effects,\ncognitive dysfunction and falls. The general treatment guidelines on hypertension\nshould be followed but it is appropriate to start with lower doses and build up. Re-\nevaluate therapy every 6-12 months because blood pressure may decrease as a\nresult of progression of atherosclerotic disease.\nDiuretics\nSince the elderly have a decreased plasma volume and lower levels of aldosterone,\naggressive diuretic therapy to reduce BP is not indicated. Even low doses may\nprecipitate hypotension, falls, hyponatraemia and hypokalaemia.\nGravitational\noedema will respond to simple mechanical measures such as raising legs and does\nnot usually warrant use of diuretics."
