        "shortTitle": "Section 4",
        "longTitle": "Digoxin",
        "text": "Digoxin\nLower maintenance doses e.g. 0.0625 to 0.125 mg (paediatric elixir formulation)\nshould be used owing to reduced renal function and increased sensitivity. Signs of\ndigoxin toxicity are nausea, vomiting, anorexia, visual disturbances and headache.\nWhere there is no evidence of heart failure and if the heart is in normal sinus\nrhythm digoxin may be safely withdrawn but the patient should be monitored for\natrial fibrillation if discontinuation is attempted.\nOral hypoglycaemics (see Diabetes section in Metabolic and Endocrine\nConditions Chapter)\nThe elderly are at an increased risk of hypoglycaemia with Glibenclamide and\nshould be prescribed Gliclazide.\nHypnotic / Sedatives\nBenzodiazepines (e.g. Diazepam) significantly impair cognitive function and\nshould not be used. Sedation should be achieved with:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nlorazepam po\nB\nE\n0.5-1mg\nat night\nintermittently\nMajor tranquillisers\nIt is essential to define and remove the underlying cause of agitation e.g. infection\nor hypoxia. Once this is done and if tranquillisation is still considered necessary,\nthe options are:\nMedicine\nCodes\nAdult dose\nFrequency\nDuration\nhaloperidol po\nB\nV\n0.5-2mg\ntwice daily\nreview\nor\nhaloperidol im\nB\nV\n1-5mg\ntwice daily\nreview\nAlways start with the lower dose if possible. Usually haloperidol 0.5 mg twice\ndaily which is often enough. Avoid chlorpromazine and fluphenazine decanoate\nwhere possible as major irreversible side effects may occur."
