        "longTitle": "LEUKOCYTE-POOR BLOOD",
        "text": "BLOOD MUST BE LESS THAN 5 DAYS OLD.\nIndications:\nDefinite clinical jaundice on Day 1 of life\nClinical signs of kernicterus\nTotal Serum Bilirubin levels as shown in the table below [26.3], depending on\ngestational and postnatal age of the baby.\nTable 26.3 Serum Bilirubin Levels and Exchange Transfusion\nGestational Age\n(Weeks)\nDay 1 of Life\nSerum Bilirubin\n(mmol/l)\nDay 2 of Life\nSerum Bilirubin (umol/l)\n>37\n350 (300 if sick and/or acidotic)\n34 to 37\n350 (270 if sick and/or acidotic)\n31 to 34\n290(240 if sick and/or acidotic)\n29 to 31\n250 (200 if sick and/or acidotic)\nLEUKOCYTE-POOR BLOOD\nOccasionally required in patients who need regular transfusion over prolonged\nperiods, in order to prevent febrile reactions due to white cell antibodies and\nprovision of CMV negative blood from unscreened blood. Bedside leukocyte\nreduction filters, supplied by the Blood Transfusion Service, may be used to attain\nthe same product.\nALBUMIN 4%\nCan be used as a volume expander and is HIV free. Must not be used if the\nsolution appears turbid or contains a deposit. Protect from light and do not freeze.\nBirth weight\nVolume of aliquots\n< 1000 – 1490g\n5ml\n1500 – 2499g\n10ml\n>2500g\n20ml\nWithdrawal of blood\n1 minute\nInjection of blood\n4 minutes"
